{"type":"assistant","message":{"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Key findings:\n- Button 1:28: black pill 160×60, white text child \"Button\" (1:29)\n- Text Field 1:31: Label text (1:32) + Input frame (1:33) with Placeholder text child. Input frame has cornerRadius presumably. fills empty on component root.\n- Logo_Foodieland: image fill FIT 282×48\n- Photos: image fill FILL (330×220)\n- Avatars: image FILL (96×96)\n- Categories/decor: image FIT (120×120)\n- Icon_Hot_Recipes: image FILL 120×120\n- Icons: vector children with #1a1a1f fills, component bg white fill\n- Workspace page 9:2, 0 children.\n\nNote: icon components have white background fills (SOLID #ffffff). When placing icons on tinted backgrounds I may want to override the instance's own fill to transparent. Icons at small sizes — white bg on white card is fine; on cyan tint it would show a white box. I'll override instance fills to [] where needed.\n\nImage hashes I have:\n- Logo: fa9f0311b756d33a88641e4f2e6668ed3340c891\n- Fried rice: a8a2f85871e0a64ae8021799559e16d6e570fab4\n- John avatar: dc78dc9ecd990b862e13fdafc6ee3b12584baa76\n- Category breakfast: cc0a6275d61aac085b89f7386aa09e2356a26650\n- Decor onion: 7035fb66419686429b96b4ad45f114602254247e\n- Hot recipes icon: 04d4082146d70e3b91b3556c67edc8404d998d21\n\nBut rather than hardcode hashes, I'll write a helper that reads the component's fills imageHash dynamically. \n\nFont check: need Inter Regular, Medium, Semi Bold, Bold. Load them in each call.\n\nNow the big build. Let me define the shared helper module as a string I paste at the top of each build call. Keep helpers concise.\n\nHelper plan:\n\n```js\nconst P = '9:2';\nconst page = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(P);\nawait figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page);\n// fonts\nconst F = { r:{family:'Inter',style:'Regular'}, m:{family:'Inter',style:'Medium'}, sb:{family:'Inter',style:'Semi Bold'}, b:{family:'Inter',style:'Bold'} };\nfor (const s of ['Regular','Medium','Semi Bold','Bold']) await figma.loadFontAsync({family:'Inter',style:s});\n\nconst C = {\n  ink:'000000', body:'4B4B52', muted:'86868F', line:'E8E8EA', tint:'E7F9FD', tintLine:'C9EBF5', white:'FFFFFF', fav:'E5484D', bg:'FFFFFF'\n};\nfunction hex(h){ return {r:parseInt(h.slice(0,2),16)/255, g:parseInt(h.slice(2,4),16)/255, b:parseInt(h.slice(4,6),16)/255}; }\nfunction solid(h,o){ return {type:'SOLID', color:hex(h), opacity:o===undefined?1:o}; }\nasync function comp(id){ return await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id); }\nfunction inst(id){ const n=comp(id); return n.createInstance? ... }\n```\n\ninst must be sync-ish: getNodeByIdAsync is async; I'll preload a dict of all needed components once:\n\n```js\nconst COMP_IDS = { logo:'7:15', fb:'7:48', tw:'7:50', ig:'7:49', ... };\nconst CM = {};\nfor (const [k,id] of Object.entries(COMP_IDS)) CM[k] = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id);\nfunction I(k){ return CM[k].createInstance(); }\nfunction paint(k, scale){ const f = CM[k].fills.find(p=>p.type==='IMAGE'); return {type:'IMAGE', imageHash:f.imageHash, scaleMode:scale||'FILL'}; }\n```\n\nText helper:\n\n```js\nfunction T(parent, chars, size, style, hexColor, opts={}) {\n  const t = figma.createText();\n  t.fontName = F[style]; t.characters = chars; t.fontSize = size;\n  t.fills = [solid(hexColor)];\n  if (opts.ls) t.letterSpacing = {unit:'PERCENT', value:opts.ls};\n  if (opts.lh) t.lineHeight = {unit:'PIXELS', value:opts.lh};\n  t.name = opts.name || (chars.slice(0,24)||'Text');\n  parent.appendChild(t);\n  if (opts.w) { t.textAutoResize='HEIGHT'; t.resize(opts.w, t.height); }\n  if (opts.align) t.textAlignHorizontal = opts.align;\n  if (opts.x!==undefined) t.x=opts.x; if (opts.y!==undefined) t.y=opts.y;\n  return t;\n}\n```\n\nNote: fontName must be set before characters; set after loadFontAsync.\n\nRect helper with paint:\n\n```js\nfunction img(parent, key, x,y,w,h, scale, radius, name){\n  const r = figma.createRectangle();\n  r.resize(w,h); r.x=x; r.y=y; r.fills=[paint(key,scale)];\n  if (radius) r.cornerRadius=radius;\n  r.name = name || ('Img_'+key);\n  parent.appendChild(r); return r;\n}\nfunction rect(parent,x,y,w,h,fillHex,radius,name){...}\nfunction frame(parent,x,y,w,h,name,fillHex,radius){...}\n```\n\nComponent creation: `figma.createComponent()`, set size, append children.\n\nPositioning: I'll build most things with absolute positioning inside fixed-size frames (no auto layout) — more predictable for pixel-perfect design. But auto layout gives better quality for text flow... Mixed: use absolute for main frames, with carefully computed positions. Text auto-resize HEIGHT with fixed width for paragraphs. Heights of blocks I'll estimate generously.\n\nActually auto layout could save a lot of math for stacked sections. But mixing AL with instance overrides is fine. However AL frames need padding/gap config; text width handling differs. Given the complexity, I'll go with absolute positioning and generous spacing, computing y offsets manually per section. Risky for overlap errors, but manageable if I keep consistent metrics and verify via screenshots.\n\nHmm — but paragraphs with textAutoResize=HEIGHT: height is computed after set; I can read t.height after setting characters to compute next y. That gives flow-like layout. I'll write a small flow stacker per section: maintain y cursor, place elements, y += elem.height + gap. Since t.height valid after set, this works.\n\nFor frames I create with h unknown: create frame, append children, then `frame.resizeWithoutConstraints(w, yCursor)` at end. I'll write helper `sect(...)` returning {f, y} closure... Let me structure per section builder functions returning total height, then position sections in the page frame sequentially.\n\nLet me now define the full spec of every frame section by section, with concrete coordinates.\n\nGLOBAL\n- Page bg default (light gray canvas fine).\n- Frames: x positions: 01_Home at (0,0); 02_Recipe (1540,0); 03_Blog (3080,0); 04_Blog_Post (4620,0); 05_Contact (6160,0). Components zone at x -26