ems to enumerate a subset. Maybe the validation here is on an older/newer schema where transition requires easing for DISSOLVE: {type:'DISSOLVE',duration:0.3,easing:{type:'EASE_OUT'}}? One branch said \"Required value missing at action.transition.easing\" + \"Invalid enum ... received 'DISSOLVE'\" — that branch expects direction-based transitions. Another branch: \"Expected null, received object at action.transition\" — that's for BACK/CLOSE (no transition allowed).\n\nHmm, the full union of action.type values in the current schema: BACK, CLOSE, URL, NODE?, CONDITIONAL, UPDATE_MEDIA_RUNTIME, SET_VARIABLE, SET_VARIABLE_MODE, CHANGE_TO?. The error didn't list a NODE branch explicitly... but wait — it DID: the branches listed are what failed; maybe NODE branch failed because transition.DISSOLVE requires 'easing' AND 'duration'... Look: there's one branch error: \"Expected action.transition to be one of the following, but none matched: Required value missing at action.transition.easing; Multiple issues: Invalid enum value. Expected 'MOVE_IN'|'MOVE_OUT'|'PUSH'|'SLIDE_IN'|'SLIDE_OUT', received 'DISSOLVE' at transition.type; Required value missing at transition.direction; Required at transition.matchLayers; Required at transition.easing\" — this branch failed on DISSOLVE.\n\nMaybe the NODE action type is actually spelled differently in this schema version... Actually I recall the Plugin API typings: Action = {type:'NODE', destinationId, navigation, transition,...}. This validation looks like the REST-like strict schema; possibly this MCP's use_figma wraps a restricted prototype API where navigate uses {type:'NODE', destinationId, navigation:'NAVIGATE', transition:{type:'DISSOLVE',duration,easing}}.\n\nBest approach: test variations quickly on the test card:\n1. trigger {type:'ON_CLICK'}, action {type:'NODE',destinationId,navigation:'NAVIGATE'} (no transition)\n2. add transition {type:'DISSOLVE',duration:0.3,easing:{type:'EASE_IN_AND_OUT'}}\n3. maybe transition {type:'SMART